<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Appian Way</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/312-bce">312 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Via Appia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Appian Way (Via Appia) was a Roman road begun in 312 BCE by the censor Appius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> Caecus, first from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Capua and later extended to Brundisium on the Adriatic. Built to move armies during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samnite-wars">Samnite Wars</a>, it became one of the main arteries of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Long stretches with their original paving and roadside tombs survive near Rome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/appian-way.jpg" alt="Appian Way" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LuisaV72 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paesaggio_dell%27Appia_antica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Appian Way was the first long road built specifically to move troops outside the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The Pontine Marshes, a malarial swamp between Rome and Capua, had defeated Roman supply in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-samnite-war">First Samnite War</a> of 343 to 341 BCE, and the Second Samnite War broke out in 327 BCE after Rome tried to plant colonies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cales">Cales</a> and Fregellae. In 312 BCE the censor Appius Claudius Caecus began public works without waiting for the Senate, among them the road, which crossed the marshes to the coast north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and then turned north to Capua. It is credited with letting Rome concentrate and supply its forces; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> came to terms in 311 BCE and Samnium in 304 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The road began as a levelled dirt path with small stones and mortar, covered with gravel and topped with tightly fitting interlocking stones, cambered for drainage and flanked by ditches. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> remarked that the stones seemed to have grown together. It is believed to be the first Roman road to use lime cement. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Forum Romanum</a> it left through the Porta Capena; across the marsh a stone causeway of about 31 kilometres needed constant repair, and in 162 BCE Marcus Cornelius Cathegus had a canal built beside it, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> preferred.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Capua was 132 Roman miles, and Capua to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a> 233 more. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sentinum">Battle of Sentinum</a> in 295 BCE the road was extended past Capua to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a>, then toward Venusia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>, and it reached Brundisium in 264 BCE. Appius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> died in 273, but no one has displaced his name from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Supplied by the road, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> crushed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a> in 275 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a> fell in 272 BCE. In 71 BCE, after the defeat of the slave revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a>, 6,000 slaves were crucified along the 200 kilometres of the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Capua. In 1943 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> landing at Anzio, meaning to advance along the line of the Via Appia to take Rome, were pinned down for four months and took Rome in May 1944.</p><p>The Roman section survives, lined with monuments of all periods, though the cement has eroded from the joints. Statius called it the queen of the long roads, and in July 2024 the Appian Way entered the UNESCO World Heritage List.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Appian Way” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its roads, beginning with the <b>Via Appia</b> of 312 BCE, its colonies and its law shaped the map and institutions of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
